sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: the proof runner inside stage 3 of the command development pipeline (design: reference/verification-cases). One shared runner executes the cases of one command; each batch restores the task's base snapshot once, cases use their own resources and undo nothing, the cases of a batch run in one step, several at a time on different Nodes; a case is a specification built from a fixed set of steps. Orbit's own Swamp runs the runner over SSH in the task VM, as part of the "Run the proof" child of proof:run. The Gateway only admits the request and records the verdicts.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: development pipeline stage 3 · proof runner
  %% kind: pipeline.3-proof-runner
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane NT icon=server pill=fleet tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>verify workflow
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>runner · over ssh
  participant NT as Nested fleet<br/>Gateway · 2 Nodes
  GW->>SW: Start the verify run<br/>cases · commit
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the commit and the one command whose cases run; in CI, `proof:run` proves each affected command separately
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the Gateway only authorizes; Swamp owns the run and its state
  SW->>VM: Select the cases<br/>runner plan
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the command's cases file at the tested commit
  %%> - refuses a case with an unknown step, an unknown chart path or no batch
  %%> - lists the chart paths, options and codes that no case covers
  %%> - groups the cases by batch, in the order of `swamp/proof/batches.yaml`; inside a batch, in file order
  %%> - `runner plan --lines` prints one line a batch, `BATCH SETUP CASE...`, which the workflow reads; the workflow `proof-fleet-verify` is built
  VM-->>SW: Ordered plan<br/>batches · cases
  loop each batch, one at a time
    SW->>NT: Restore base<br/>snapshot and start · about 16 s
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - restores the task's plain snapshot `base` of the three nested containers (a reboot); skipped when the fleet is still at `base`, as for the first batch
    %%> - a restore that fails or is not healthy within 45 s stops the run; the cases that did not run become `unrun` with cause `infrastructure`
    %%> - built, not yet passed live: the step is the workflow `proof-fleet-restore` (restore, start, boot wait, readiness, apply the checkout), which `proof-fleet-verify-batch` calls; 0 of 2 runs succeeded by 2026-11-06; the old `runner restore` was removed
    opt the workflow calls setup
      SW->>VM: Prepare and set up<br/>runner setup BATCH
      %%> New workflows always call setup. Older released workflows may skip it for a batch without setup steps.
      VM->>NT: Apply Gateway<br/>prerequisites
      VM->>NT: Prepare the observers<br/>one Ansible run
      %%> Installs the candidate pin using Node convergence's checksum-verified atomic replacement task. Stops all observers, discards old host receipts, starts the observers and waits for the Gateway to record each expected version plus a new host fact. No new base snapshot.
      %%> Preparation has its own 330-second bound. Setup gets a separate 120 seconds within the 480-second transport limit.
      opt the batch declares a start state
        VM->>NT: Set up the batch<br/>batch setup steps
      end
    end
    SW->>VM: Run the batch<br/>runner batch BATCH
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one Swamp step runs every case of the batch; the runner reads the fleet once
    %%> - the cases that share the fleet run several at a time (the `parallel` key of the cases file, 4 by default), never two on one Node: each case acts on one Node of the cases file's `spread`, and the Gateway lets one firewall write run on a Node at a time
    %%> - the cases that change or compare what every case shares (the Operation list, a fault, a held lock) run alone, after the others
    %%> - no restore after a failed case: cases use their own resources, undo nothing, and the next batch restores anyway
    %%> Result:
    %%> - one verdict line a case, those that did not pass first; the full receipt of each stays in the evidence directory
    VM->>NT: Apply Gateway<br/>prerequisites
    VM->>NT: Prepare the observers<br/>one Ansible run
    %%> The batch always prepares independently, even if setup succeeded or an older workflow skipped setup. A preparation failure prints infrastructure failures and starts no case. Direct runner case calls require the same preparation.
    loop each case, several at a time
      VM->>NT: Set up the case<br/>listener · ufw_rule · probe
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - builds the starting state, then probes it, for example a blocked port and a working control port
      %%> - a bound firewall anchor matches the current case's tuple and comment for the same protocol, so a repeated comment from an earlier case or another protocol cannot select its rule
      %%> When:
      %%> - a failed setup step ends the case as `unrun`; it is not a pass
      VM->>NT: Run the real CLI<br/>built from the commit
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - the `act` steps call the command through its real interface and check exit status, result code and skip reason
      VM->>NT: Check the effect<br/>probe · ufw · record · doctor
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - the `expect` steps check traffic, rule order, the Gateway record and Doctor
      %%> - every step runs, so one verdict lists all failures
    end
    VM-->>SW: Verdicts<br/>one JSON line a case
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the case id, `passed`, `failed` (cause `assertion` or `infrastructure`) or `unrun` (cause `precondition`), the failed checks and the seconds
    %%> - Swamp cuts a step's output at 4096 bytes: the runner prints the lines that did not pass first, and counts the lines it had to leave out; a case without a line is reported as having printed no verdict
  end
  alt every case passed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>verdicts + receipt
    Note over GW: Record the result once<br/>PASS
  else a case failed or did not run
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed cases
    Note over GW: Record the result once<br/>FAIL
  end
